// A `let rec` INSIDE A REGION BLOCK INSIDE A FUNCTION, which the LLVM backend emitted
// as invalid IR for five versions.
//
// v0.1.453 gave a lifted inner function its enclosing region as a leading parameter, so
// that a body called from a nested block allocates in the block it was WRITTEN in rather
// than the one that happens to be current (Q-131). The parameter is named `__region_R`,
// and on the C backend that name is also what the block's arena local is called -- so
// emitting the capture's own name as the argument happened to name the right thing.
//
// LLVM names the block's arena `%t7`, or whatever `fresh_reg` produced. Emitting
// `%__region_R` there produced `use of undefined value` -- INVALID IR, so this program
// did not build at all on that backend while building and running fine on the other
// three. `mere -ll` still exited 0; only the assembler said anything, and only if
// somebody ran it.
//
// The existing region/inner-fn parity cases did not catch it because their blocks are at
// the top level, where the two naming conventions coincide. This one puts the block
// inside a function, which is the shape that separates them. Found by poisoning
// region_reclaim_check -- the poison was supposed to test the gate, and indicted the
// backend instead.
let run = fn (d: int) ->
  region R {
    let v = vec_new () in
    let rec f = fn (j: int) -> if j >= d then 0 else let _ = vec_push v j in f (j + 1) in
    let _ = f 0 in
    vec_len v
  };

let _ = print_int (run 5);
print_int (run 12)
